            <ArticleTitle>Metatarsophalangeal Joint Fusion: Why and How?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>465-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fcl.2015.04.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1083-7515(15)00035-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>First metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint fusion aims at elimination of pain resulting from end-stage arthritis and obtaining a stable, plantigrade first toe. Associated deformities are corrected and greater defects are filled with interposition autograft or allograft. Fusion is generally obtained with screws, staples, and/or low-profile plates. Complications include infection, osteonecrosis, implant protrusion or failure, nonunion, and malunion, the latter 2 each occurring in approximately 6% of cases. The medium-term results of first MTP joint fusion indicate mostly good functional results with success rates of approximately 90%.</AbstractText>
